{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And I said to you at that time, saying.</b> 'At that time' points to the time when he said to them: 'Come and possess the land' — for I saw that at that time there was an urgent need for the appointment of judges, for this merit would suffice to seat you upon the land, as it is written (Deuteronomy 16:20): ״צֶדֶק צֶדֶק תִּרְדֹּף לְמַעַן תִּחְיֶה וְיָרַשְׁתָּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ״, which Rashi explained: the appointment of qualified judges is worthy to give life to Israel and to seat them upon their land. And so we explained above in parashat Yitro (Exodus 18:23), the verse 'and all this people shall come to their place in peace' — since it does not say 'יֵשֵׁב בְּשָׁלוֹם,' infer from this that it speaks of a place to which they have not yet come; and since it does not say 'אִישׁ עַל מְקֹמוֹ,' infer from this that it speaks of the place designated for the entire people as a whole, and this is the whole land of Israel in general, which all Israel would inherit through the merit of appointing judges. Therefore he said: 'Take for yourselves men' — even if you should say that you would not wish to do so for my sake, to lighten my burden, nevertheless do it for yourselves, for your own benefit, in order that you may inherit the land. And furthermore it is written (Exodus 18:18): 'נָבֹל תִּבֹּל גַּם אַתָּה גַּם הָעָם' — therefore it says 'take for yourselves' — if not for my sake." "And regarding what is said, 'I am not able to bear you alone,' and afterward he said, 'How can I alone bear your burden,' etc. — one may explain it in two ways. The first is that the expression 'I am not able' denotes a thing that is prevented and entirely impossible, while the expression 'How can I bear' denotes a thing that is possible, only that it is not proper to do it. And 'I am not able' also carries an implication for the future, for at that time, in the place where he was, the thing was possible and not prevented, as Rashi explained: Is it possible that Moses was not able to judge Israel — the man who brought them out of Egypt, etc.? And if so, in that generation, according to the number that they were at that time, he was certainly able, but if 'HaShem add to you a thousand times as many as you are,' then the thing would certainly become impossible. And when he said 'How can I alone bear,' he said this concerning that time — although it is possible, nevertheless it is not proper. And when he said 'I am not able,' he said in the end that for the future I will not be able to bear it alone, and he gave a reason for the matter: for now 'HaShem your Elohim has multiplied you, and behold, you are today as the stars of the heavens for multitude' — and your merit alone, from the aspect that He is your Elohim, has established you at this measure of number, and that amount I can bear. However, when the merit of the fathers is joined to this as well, from the aspect that He is the Elohim of your fathers, then surely 'He will add to you a thousand times as many as you are,' etc. And concerning that time which is destined to come at any moment, I say in conclusion that I will not be able at all, and even now it is not proper; and concerning the present I say, 'How can I alone bear,' etc." "The second: I agree with Rashi's explanation, who explained 'HaShem your Elohim has multiplied you' — He took the punishment from you and placed it upon the judges. And the judgment is true judgment, for this one who holds money unjustly believes that the law is with him, since the judge so rules for him — behold, it is truly his, and therefore it is just that the judgment stands and the judge bears this one's burden. And it appears that therefore he said: 'and behold, you are today as the stars of the heavens' — for they endure, and this endurance comes to you from the aspect that HaShem your Elohim has multiplied and made you great and has placed your punishment upon the judges. And concerning this he said also: 'and I shall set them at your head' — for the guilt of the people hangs everywhere upon their heads, for the matter of judges is a root principle for the entire Torah. And concerning the punishment he said: 'I am not able to bear you alone' — he did not say 'to bear your burden and your load,' because one who bears this one's punishment is as though he bears his very person upon himself, and the punishment I cannot bear at all, for 'HaShem your Elohim has multiplied you,' etc., as Rashi explained. But afterward he spoke concerning the bearing of their burden and their load and their strife — although it is possible, nevertheless it is not proper; and concerning this he said: 'How can I alone bear,' etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:14.307082208Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}